  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Account Manipulation — Additional Cloud Roles", "technique": "T1098.003", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 10:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's cloud-audit module logged an Azure AD directory-role mutation. A bearer token belonging to the standard user 'alice.jones' (no prior IAM-mutation history in 90 days) was used from data.srcip=91.211.247.155 to drive an 'Add member to role' operation, assigning principal a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890 to the Global Administrator role (roleTemplateId 62e90394-69f5-4237-9190-012177145e10). A second mutation attempted an Owner roleAssignment on a subscription. Wazuh fired custom rule 92320 ('Azure: Add member to Global Administrator role') at level 14. The actor account is a normal user — it should never perform a directory-role grant — and the source IP is not on the corporate Privileged Access Workstation list.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92320 fired with rule.groups containing 'azure' and 'authentication_success', rule.description matched 'Add member to Global Administrator role' naming the actor alice.jones and the targeted role, rule.mitre.id contained T1098.003, and data.srcip=91.211.247.155 was attached as the initiating address. The univariate signal: a non-PAW source driving a Global Administrator role grant by an account with zero 90-day IAM-mutation baseline.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Account Manipulation — Additional Cloud Roles", "technique": "T1098.003", "what_happened": "At 10:07:30 CEST (~5 minutes later), the cloud-audit module logged an AWS IAM mutation from the same data.srcip=91.211.247.155: an AttachUserPolicy operation attaching the managed policy arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess to the IAM user 'svc-bot', immediately followed by a CreateAccessKey call minting fresh long-lived programmatic credentials for that same user. Wazuh fired custom rule 92321 ('AWS: AdministratorAccess policy attached to user') at level 14. The AttachUserPolicy + CreateAccessKey pairing is the canonical cloud-persistence move — the new access key survives a console password reset of svc-bot.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92321 fired with rule.groups containing 'aws', rule.description matched 'AdministratorAccess policy attached to user' naming the target svc-bot, rule.mitre.id contained T1098.003, and data.srcip=91.211.247.155 matched the same external source as step 1. The AttachUserPolicy-then-CreateAccessKey sequence on a service account from a non-corporate IP is the high-fidelity AWS privilege-grant-plus-persistence signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:07:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Account Manipulation — Additional Cloud Roles", "technique": "T1098.003", "what_happened": "From 10:11:30 to 10:14:55 CEST, the on-prem Active Directory leg fired. The Domain Controller at 10.108.57.10 logged Security Event ID 4728 — 'A member was added to a security-enabled global group' — recording that 'svc.bot' was added to the 'Domain Admins' group, preceded by an LDAP enumeration of the Domain Admins membership (Security Event ID 4662) from the same operator. Wazuh fired custom rule 4728 ('AD: member added to Domain Admins (EID 4728)') at level 14. This completes a three-plane privilege-escalation chain: Azure Global Administrator + AWS AdministratorAccess + on-prem Domain Admins, all within ~13 minutes.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 4728 fired with rule.groups containing 'windows' and 'authentication', rule.description matched 'member added to Domain Admins' naming svc.bot as the added member, rule.mitre.id contained T1098.003, and agent.ip=10.108.57.10 identified the Domain Controller. The 4662-then-4728 sequence (enumerate the group, then add to it) within the same window as the Azure and AWS grants is the on-prem completion of a coordinated cross-plane privilege grant.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:11:30+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Privilege Escalation / Anomalous Source Context", "technique": "T1098.003", "what_happened": "Across the 10:02-10:16 window, Wazuh's UEBA correlation flagged that the actor 'alice.jones' had performed ZERO IAM or directory mutations in the preceding 90 days, and that every grant in the chain originated from data.srcip=91.211.247.155 — an address absent from the Privileged Access Workstation allowlist and absent from the corporate egress ranges. Wazuh fired custom rule 92322 ('Priv grant from non-PAW workstation') at level 12. The combination — first-ever privileged action by the actor + non-PAW source + three privileged grants in 14 minutes — is the behavioural anomaly that elevates this from 'admin activity' to 'account-takeover-driven privilege escalation'.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92322 fired with rule.description matching 'Priv grant from non-PAW workstation' and data.srcip=91.211.247.155, correlating the actor's zero-baseline with the non-allowlisted source across all three preceding grant events. The anomaly rule is the correlation glue binding the Azure, AWS and AD legs into one coordinated T1098.003 campaign.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:16:00+0200"}
